1
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,300
I've seen my own death.
Not once. Three times.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,200
Each time in a different city.
Each time in a different year.

3
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:14,900
The first was in Berlin, 1987.
I was just a child.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,600
The second was here in Chicago.
Last Tuesday, to be exact.

5
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,400
And the third...
the third hasn't happened yet.

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:26,500
Dr. Rivera, you're saying you
actually remember these events?

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,200
Not just remember. I lived through them.
Every detail. Every sound. Every face.

8
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:34,000
But that's impossible. You can't
experience an event before it occurs.

9
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,900
That's what we thought before
the Continuum device.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,700
[device humming loudly]
It's active. The field is stable.

11
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,600
Stable? The last test overloaded
three city blocks. Define stable.

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,400
Contained and controllable.
We solved the cascade problem.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,300
How? We've been trying for seven years.
What changed overnight?

14
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:57,000
Not overnight. I went back.
I fixed the mistake before it happened.

15
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,900
You used the prototype without
authorization. You could have been killed.

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,700
I was killed. In one timeline.
That's what I've been trying to tell you.

17
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,500
And you just... overwrote that
timeline? Replaced it with this one?

18
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,300
<i>The timeline doesn't get overwritten.
It branches. Every choice creates a fork.</i>

19
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,100
<i>Somewhere out there, in branch delta-7,
the cascade failure still happened.</i>

20
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,900
<i>That version of me died in the lab fire.
This version is standing here talking to you.</i>

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,800
That's horrifying. You're saying
there's a dead version of you out there?

22
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,600
Many dead versions. Time travel
doesn't defeat death. It multiplies it.

23
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,500
Then why do it? Why risk
fracturing reality itself?

24
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,300
Because Sarah is still alive
in at least four of those branches.

25
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:39,100
(voice breaking)
And I intend to find a branch where
she stays that way.

26
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,900
Elena, that's not science anymore.
That's grief. That's dangerous.

27
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:46,800
All the greatest discoveries were
dangerous. Fire. The atom. Now this.

28
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,600
Director Marsh wants this project
shut down by Tuesday.

29
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,400
By Tuesday. Interesting.
He said that last Tuesday too.

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,300
What does that mean?
Elena, what did you change?

31
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:02,100
♪ tense string music ♪

32
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,900
I changed enough so that we'd
have until Friday. That's all you need.

33
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,700
Friday for what?
What isn't she telling you?

34
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,600
The attractor state. There's a point,
about 72 hours from now...

35
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,400
...where every surviving branch
converges on the same event.

36
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,300
What event? You're frightening me,
and I am not easy to frighten.

37
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:25,100
All branches. All versions of us.
They all disappear simultaneously.

38
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:28,900
That's not possible. Branches are
independent. They can't all collapse.

39
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,800
They can if something is erasing
them from the outside.

40
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,600
Outside of what? Outside of time?
That doesn't make sense.

41
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,500
It makes perfect sense if someone
else has also built a Continuum device.

42
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:44,400
Someone who, unlike you,
is using it as a weapon.

43
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,300
Who would do that? Who even
knows this technology exists?

44
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:52,100
We didn't work alone.
You know who helped us build the first prototype.

45
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:55,900
Marcus. You're saying Marcus
has gone rogue with our own technology.

46
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,800
He didn't go rogue. He never agreed
with our goals from the beginning.

47
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,600
He wanted to erase mistakes
from history. Entire events. Entire people.

48
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,500
And we stopped him. The ethics
board ruled his proposals genocidal.

49
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:11,400
We voted. He lost.
So he built his own device and voted differently.

50
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,300
Then we have 72 hours to find him
before all of this stops existing.

